course: arts
lesson: 25
title: Special effects and stunts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Special effects are tricks that make impossible things look real on screen: flying heroes, dragons, sinking ships and monsters.
explain: Some tricks are done on set with models, make-up, rubber suits and careful camera angles; others are added later on computers, like replacing a big green wall with a whole city.
explain: Dangerous-looking moments are planned for weeks by trained stunt performers and safety teams with padding, airbags and harnesses you never see, and even they sometimes get hurt, so stunts are only ever for professionals, never for copying at home.
explain: Once you know how a monster was made, it gets much less scary: watch a behind-the-scenes clip and the beast is a man in a rubber suit having a cup of tea.
explain: People say it their own way: in Britain "that's so jokes" means really funny, "mad money" is a huge amount of money, and "don't you dare" from a mum means a firm no.
fact: Actors are often filmed in front of a green screen, and computers later replace the green with a background like a city or space.
fact: Wires can hold actors up safely, and the wires are removed later by computer.
fact: Stunt performers are trained professionals who rehearse with safety teams and hidden safety gear, and they can still get hurt; film stunts must never be copied. A stunt double is a trained stunt performer who stands in for an actor in the dangerous moments.
fact: When a creature will be added by computer later, actors look at a tennis ball on a stick or a person in a grey suit so their eyes point the right way.
fact: In the 1933 film King Kong the giant gorilla was a small model moved by hand a tiny bit at a time.
fact: Film fights are planned so the punches miss; the camera angle, the sound effect and the other actor's reaction make it look like a hit.
fact: In black-and-white films, chocolate syrup was used as fake blood because it looked right on screen.
fact: Some films use real controlled explosions, handled only by licensed experts with strict safety rules, while others add explosions by computer.
fact: To show an actor much younger, films use make-up, a younger actor, or computers, which is called de-ageing; some viewers love it and others say it looks strange, so it is argued.
fact: For Titanic in 1997, the filmmakers built a large part of the ship and used models, water tanks and computer effects instead of sinking a real ship.
fact: Behind-the-scenes videos show how effects are made, and that can help children feel less scared of film monsters.
exercise: How is a superhero often made to fly in a film? | On wires before a green screen | The actor really learns to fly after months of special training | The camera is thrown high into the air to make the actor look as if flying | A crane lifts a real city
exercise: Amelia's brother wants to jump off the garage like the stuntman. What is right? | No, stunts are for trained professionals | Yes, as long as he bends his knees and lands on his feet like in the film | Yes, but only from a small garage roof with a pillow on the ground below | Yes, it looked easy
exercise: Why do actors look at a tennis ball on a stick? | It marks where a computer creature will be | It is a game the crew plays to keep the actors awake between long scenes | The ball is used to hold the camera steady while it films the actors | It makes actors braver
exercise: How was the old King Kong gorilla made? | A small model moved by hand | A real gorilla that was trained for years to climb buildings | A very tall man on stilts wearing a hairy gorilla suit | An early computer program built specially for the film in 1933
exercise: Rahul is sure the punch hit. What really happens in film fights? | The punch misses on purpose | The actors really do hit each other, only a little bit softer than usual | The actors wear thin invisible armour so they can punch each other hard | Every fight in every film is drawn in later by computer, not acted
exercise: What was fake blood made of in old black-and-white films? | Chocolate syrup | Real blood from a hospital | Red paint mixed with sand | Tomato soup only
exercise: Kai's mate says they blew up a real car. What is true? | Sometimes real, sometimes computer | Always real, and anyone on the film crew is allowed to do it | Never real, because explosions of any kind are banned in films | Only toy cars are used
exercise: Who handles explosions in films? | Only licensed experts with strict rules | The youngest actor on set | Anybody with a lighter | The cinema staff
exercise: How can an actor look 30 years younger in a flashback? | Computers, make-up or another actor | The actor had surgery to look young for one scene and then changed back | Old films of the actor from thirty years ago are always used instead | The camera does it alone
exercise: Do all viewers like de-ageing? | No, it is argued | Yes, everyone agrees that it looks absolutely perfect every time | No, because it is against the law in most countries to use it | Yes, because nobody watching the film can ever notice it at all
exercise: How was the Titanic sinking filmed in 1997? | Part of a ship, models, tanks and computers | By sinking a real old ship out in the middle of the ocean | By sending cameras down to film the real Titanic underwater | By filming a bathtub boat
exercise: Freya's brother is scared of a film monster. What can help? | Showing him the man in the suit | Telling him that monsters are real but live very far away | Making him watch the scary film again by himself to get used to it | Turning the sound up
exercise: What is a green screen for? | Computers replace the green with another background | It keeps the actors cool | It is the colour that cameras cannot see | It makes the actors look taller
exercise: What is a stunt double? | A trained stand-in for stunts | An actor's twin brother or sister who learns all the same lines | A second copy of the film made for showing in another country | A second camera on the set that films the scene from behind
exercise: What happens to the wires holding up an actor? | They are removed later by computer | The audience is told to ignore them | They are painted green on purpose | They are left in for safety
test: Your mate goes, "If it looks easy in the film, I can do it off the shed, sorted." What is the right answer? | No, stunts need training and gear | Yes, because films only ever show things that are safe for anyone | Yes, as long as he watches the scene ten times first to learn it | Only if a friend films it so he can see what went wrong after
test: What would happen if a film fight used real punches? | Actors would get hurt | The fight would look exactly the same but somehow be much safer | The film would be banned from every cinema in the country | The actors would quickly get used to it and stop feeling the punches
test: A kid in Liverpool says, "Old effects were dead rubbish, la" ("la" means mate). What is fair to say? | They were clever for their time | They were rubbish and nobody uses any of them in films today | Old films had no special effects of any kind at all | Old effects were secretly made by early computers in the 1930s
test: In a cartoon, a boy copies a film explosion with fireworks in his garden. What should he have known? | Explosions are only for licensed experts with strict rules | Fireworks are safe if you are careful | Film explosions are made by kids | It is fine if a grown-up is watching from inside
test: Your cousin from Ohio says Titanic's crew "sank a real ship, no cap". Which is true? | They built part of it and used models | They sank a real old ship in the Atlantic Ocean to film it | They filmed the real Titanic as it was sinking back in 1912 | A bathtub and a toy boat
test: Your little brother asks why the dragon looked at the actor but the actor looked at a ball. What happened? | The dragon was added later by computer | The actor was too scared to look at the real dragon on set | The dragon was invisible on that day and came back the next | The ball was its baby
test: Grace says fake blood is "ew but yum". Why might old fake blood be yummy? | It was chocolate syrup, which looked right in black and white | It was real blood mixed with sugar | Actors liked to drink it for fun | It was strawberry jam on every film
test: A friend says a fight "deffo landed" ("deffo" means definitely). What makes a missed punch look real? | Angle, sound and reaction | A real hit that is made very soft so nobody gets hurt | Slowing down the film so the fist seems to touch the face | Hiding a thick pillow inside the actor's shirt and jacket
test: Your mum says de-ageing is "a bit creepy, like a waxwork". Which is true? | People argue about it; some love it and some find it strange | Everyone agrees it looks perfectly real | De-ageing is illegal in films | It is only used in cartoons
test: Which is the best reason stunt performers get paid as experts? | They train and rehearse with safety teams | They are not afraid of anything at all, so they need no practice | They are actors who forgot their lines and were given stunts instead | They skip all planning
test: Why might seeing a man in a rubber suit help a scared child? | It shows the monster was made by people, not real | It makes the monster even scarier | It proves monsters live in film studios | It teaches him to make his own monster suit
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the replication of cinematic stunts by members of the public is to be avoided. | They rely on training and unseen safety equipment | Stunts are invariably free from all risk and therefore require no imitation | Stunts are prohibited by law only within the premises of cinemas | Stunts are computer-made
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the function of a green screen? | A background subsequently replaced by computer with another setting | A protective barrier preventing injury to actors | A colour rendering performers taller upon screen | A signal to the audience that a scene has ended
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the film "King Kong" of 1933. | The gorilla was a small hand-moved model | The gorilla was a trained animal of very great size brought from abroad | The gorilla was generated by an early computer built for the purpose | It was a man on stilts
exam: Ascertain the procedure by which filmed combat is rendered convincing without injury. | Blows are directed to miss, with angle, sound and reaction conveying contact | Performers strike one another with reduced force | Performers wear concealed armour and strike with full force | All combat is generated subsequently by computer
